==English==

===Etymology===
A twentieth-century borrowing of {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|ἐμπάθεια|tr=empatheia|lang=grc|lit=passion|sc=polytonic}} (formed from {{term|ἐν|tr=en-||in, at|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}} + {{term|πάθος|tr=pathos||feeling|lang=grc}}), coined by [[w:Edward B. Titchener|Edward Bradford Titchener]] to translate German ''[[Einfühlung]]''. The modern Greek word {{term|εμπάθεια|lang=el}} has an opposite meaning denoting strong negative feelings and prejudice against someone.

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|~|empathies}}

# the intellectual identification of the thoughts, feelings, or state of another person
# capacity to understand another person's [[point of view]] or the result of such understanding
#: ''She had a lot of '''empathy''' for her neighbor; she knew what it was like to lose a parent too.''
# {{context|parapsychology|science fiction|lang=en}} a [[paranormal]] ability to [[psychically]] read another person's emotions

====Usage notes====
Used similarly to {{term|sympathy|lang=en}}, interchangeably in looser usage. In stricter usage, '''empathy''' is stronger and more intimate, meaning that the subject understands and shares an emotion with the object – as in “I feel your pain” – while {{term||sympathy}} is weaker and more distant – concern, but not shared emotion: “I care for you”.

